course: dictionary
lesson: 13757
title: Words with two meanings: intern
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words look the same but mean very different things. The word intern is one of them. Context tells you which meaning the author wants. Look at the words around it first, then decide.
explain: «Sam is a summer intern at the newspaper, learning to write news stories.» The words summer, learning and newspaper are your clues. A summer intern is a young person who works somewhere for a short time to learn the job, so that is the meaning here.
explain: «During the war, the government chose to intern people from the enemy country.» Here intern is a verb, and the clues are war and camp. To intern someone means to lock them up, usually in a camp. Check it by swapping in your meaning: "chose to lock people up from the enemy country" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: An intern is a student or young person who works somewhere for a short time to learn a job, sometimes without pay.
fact: «An internship» is the time that an intern spends learning a job, such as «a summer internship».
fact: In the US, an intern can also be a young doctor who has just finished medical school and is training in a hospital.
fact: As a verb, «to intern» means to lock someone up, usually in a camp during a war, because they come from an enemy country.
fact: In history lessons, you may read about people who were interned in camps during the Second World War.
exercise: «Sam is a summer intern at the newspaper.» What does intern mean here? | A person who locks people up in a camp | A holiday at the seaside | A famous writer of news stories | *A young person learning a job for a short time
exercise: In «Sam is a summer intern at the newspaper», which word tells you the job only lasts a short time? | Sam | *summer | intern | newspaper
exercise: «During the war, the government chose to intern people from the enemy country.» What does intern mean here? | To teach people a new job | To thank people for their help | *To lock people up in a camp | To give people a summer holiday
test: What is an internship? | A long holiday at the seaside | *A short time spent learning a job, often done by a young person | A test at the end of the school year | A prize for winning a race
test: Which sentence uses intern to mean lock up? | Ella is an intern at the local hospital. | The guard will go home at five o'clock. | My cousin is a summer intern in the office. | *The soldiers will intern the enemy workers in the camp.
exam: Read this: «Ella is an intern at the newspaper, and she learns to write news stories.» What might Ella be doing at the newspaper? | Building a camp for the newspaper team | *Learning to write news stories while working there for a short time | Selling newspapers on a street corner | Locking up the newspaper editor
